<div class="qandaset block" id="av_1.0-ch04_s05_s05_s02_qs02_ans">
<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Book:_General_Chemistry:_Principles_Patterns_and_Applications_(Averill)/08:_Ionic_versus_Covalent_Bonding/8.05:_Lewis_Structures" class="mt-section"><span id="Answers"></span><h3 class="lt-chem-349446 editable">Answers</h3>

<ol class="qandadiv">
    <li class="qandaentry lt-chem-349446" id="av_1.0-ch04_s05_s05_s02_qs02_qd01_qa01_ans">
    <div class="answer">
    

    <ol>
        <li class="lt-chem-349446">[Ar]4<em>s</em><sup>2</sup>3<em>d</em><sup>10</sup>4<em>p</em><sup>4</sup>

        <div class="informalfigure medium"><a class="thumb" title="" rel="internal" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/28133/d28dcbab6cd7610ccfea6219a2ff2cc4.jpg?revision=1"><img style="width: 350px; height: 44px;" width="350px" height="44px" class="internal" alt="d28dcbab6cd7610ccfea6219a2ff2cc4.jpg" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/28133/d28dcbab6cd7610ccfea6219a2ff2cc4.jpg?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=350&amp;height=44" /></a></div>

        <p class="para lt-chem-349446">Selenium can accommodate two more electrons, giving the Se<sup>2&minus;</sup> ion.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="lt-chem-349446">[Ar]4<em>s</em><sup>2</sup>3<em>d</em><sup>10</sup>4<em>p</em><sup>6</sup>

        <div class="informalfigure medium"><a class="thumb" title="" rel="internal" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/28134/d12521d5d9fdd9a94e98f69e6c0b4b56.jpg?revision=1"><img style="width: 350px; height: 44px;" width="350px" height="44px" class="internal" alt="d12521d5d9fdd9a94e98f69e6c0b4b56.jpg" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/28134/d12521d5d9fdd9a94e98f69e6c0b4b56.jpg?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=350&amp;height=44" /></a></div>

        <p class="para lt-chem-349446">Krypton has a closed shell electron configuration, so it cannot accommodate any additional electrons.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="lt-chem-349446">1<em>s</em><sup>2</sup>2<em>s</em><sup>1</sup>

        <div class="informalfigure medium"><a class="thumb" title="" rel="internal" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/28135/b51e83e711bfc4696e698d6b8e303cb6.jpg?revision=1"><img style="width: 350px; height: 34px;" width="350px" height="34px" class="internal" alt="b51e83e711bfc4696e698d6b8e303cb6.jpg" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/28135/b51e83e711bfc4696e698d6b8e303cb6.jpg?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=350&amp;height=34" /></a></div>

        <p class="para lt-chem-349446">Lithium can accommodate one additional electron in its 2<em>s</em> orbital, giving the Li<sup>&minus;</sup> ion.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="lt-chem-349446">[Kr]5<em>s</em><sup>2</sup>

        <div class="informalfigure medium"><a class="thumb" title="" rel="internal" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/28136/f1677c779cfc1a3030fd6d6f367ec45f.jpg?revision=1"><img style="width: 350px; height: 30px;" width="350px" height="30px" class="internal" alt="f1677c779cfc1a3030fd6d6f367ec45f.jpg" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/28136/f1677c779cfc1a3030fd6d6f367ec45f.jpg?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=350&amp;height=30" /></a></div>

        <p class="para lt-chem-349446">Strontium has a filled 5<em>s</em> subshell, and additional electrons would have to be placed in an orbital with a higher energy. Thus strontium has no tendency to accept an additional electron.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="lt-chem-349446">1<em>s</em><sup>1</sup>

        <div class="informalfigure medium"><a class="thumb" title="" rel="internal" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/28137/c3043255efba55862427c39dc2530bd7.jpg?revision=1"><img style="width: 350px; height: 36px;" width="350px" height="36px" class="internal" alt="c3043255efba55862427c39dc2530bd7.jpg" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/28137/c3043255efba55862427c39dc2530bd7.jpg?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=350&amp;height=36" /></a></div>

        <p class="para lt-chem-349446">Hydrogen can accommodate one additional electron in its 1<em>s</em> orbital, giving the H<sup>&minus;</sup> ion.</p>
        </li>
    </ol>
    </div>
    </li>
