    <li class="qandaentry lt-chem-349446" id="av_1.0-ch04_s05_s05_s02_qs02_qd01_qa11_ans">
    <div class="answer">
    

    

    <ol>
        <li class="lt-chem-349446">
        <div class="informalfigure medium"><a class="thumb" title="" rel="internal" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/28144/255eaaee720a80470c9015dccd15513d.jpg?revision=1"><img style="width: 241px; height: 38px;" width="241px" height="38px" class="internal" alt="255eaaee720a80470c9015dccd15513d.jpg" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/28144/255eaaee720a80470c9015dccd15513d.jpg?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=241&amp;height=38" /></a></div>

        <p class="para lt-chem-349446">K is the reductant; S is the oxidant. The final stoichiometry is K<sub>2</sub>S.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="lt-chem-349446">
        <div class="informalfigure medium"><a class="thumb" title="" rel="internal" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/28145/2e7216eec579404b3a544a7df9cf6d9f.jpg?revision=1"><img style="width: 239px; height: 37px;" width="239px" height="37px" class="internal" alt="2e7216eec579404b3a544a7df9cf6d9f.jpg" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/28145/2e7216eec579404b3a544a7df9cf6d9f.jpg?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=239&amp;height=37" /></a></div>

        <p class="para lt-chem-349446">Sr is the reductant; Br is the oxidant. The final stoichiometry is SrBr<sub>2</sub>.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="lt-chem-349446">
        <div class="informalfigure medium"><a class="thumb" title="" rel="internal" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/28146/8081bb18ebbf3999ae6d64a0e61e851b.jpg?revision=1"><img style="width: 239px; height: 37px;" width="239px" height="37px" class="internal" alt="8081bb18ebbf3999ae6d64a0e61e851b.jpg" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/28146/8081bb18ebbf3999ae6d64a0e61e851b.jpg?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=239&amp;height=37" /></a></div>

        <p class="para lt-chem-349446">Al is the reductant; O is the oxidant. The final stoichiometry is Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="lt-chem-349446">
        <div class="informalfigure medium"><a class="thumb" title="" rel="internal" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/28147/fff33ba7e50ec106710b58c3fdbe10c6.jpg?revision=1"><img style="width: 240px; height: 38px;" width="240px" height="38px" class="internal" alt="fff33ba7e50ec106710b58c3fdbe10c6.jpg" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/28147/fff33ba7e50ec106710b58c3fdbe10c6.jpg?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=240&amp;height=38" /></a></div>

        <p class="para lt-chem-349446">Mg is the reductant; Cl is the oxidant. The final stoichiometry is MgCl<sub>2</sub>.</p>
        </li>
    </ol>
    </div>
    </li>
    <li class="qandaentry lt-chem-349446" id="av_1.0-ch04_s05_s05_s02_qs02_qd01_qa12_ans"> </li>
    <li class="qandaentry lt-chem-349446" id="av_1.0-ch04_s05_s05_s02_qs02_qd01_qa13_ans"> </li>
    <li class="qandaentry lt-chem-349446" id="av_1.0-ch04_s05_s05_s02_qs02_qd01_qa14_ans"> </li>
    <li class="qandaentry lt-chem-349446" id="av_1.0-ch04_s05_s05_s02_qs02_qd01_qa15_ans">
    <div class="answer">
    <p class="para lt-chem-349446">The only structure that gives both oxygen and carbon an octet of electrons is the following:</p>

    <div class="informalfigure medium"><a class="thumb" title="CH3 bound to OH with a single bond. The oxygen has four valence electrons." rel="internal" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/28148/fb49f495d893f267e1f65c689433995e.jpg?revision=1"><img alt="CH3 bound to OH with a single bond. The oxygen has four valence electrons." style="width: 241px; height: 79px;" width="241px" height="79px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/28148/fb49f495d893f267e1f65c689433995e.jpg?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=241&amp;height=79" /></a></div>
    </div>
    </li>
    <li class="qandaentry lt-chem-349446" id="av_1.0-ch04_s05_s05_s02_qs02_qd01_qa16_ans"> </li>
    <li class="qandaentry lt-chem-349446" id="av_1.0-ch04_s05_s05_s02_qs02_qd01_qa17_ans">
    <div class="answer">
    <p class="para lt-chem-349446">The student&rsquo;s proposed structure has two flaws: the hydrogen atom with the double bond has four valence electrons (H can only accommodate two electrons), and the carbon bound to oxygen only has six valence electrons (it should have an octet). An acceptable Lewis structure is</p>

    <div class="informalfigure medium"><a class="thumb" title="The oxygen is double bound to carbon and has four valence electrons." rel="internal" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/28149/84f44017cc1a54f4f2749aa296f21283.jpg?revision=1"><img alt="The oxygen is double bound to carbon and has four valence electrons." style="width: 241px; height: 89px;" width="241px" height="89px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/28149/84f44017cc1a54f4f2749aa296f21283.jpg?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=241&amp;height=89" /></a></div>

    <p class="para lt-chem-349446">The formal charges on the correct and incorrect structures are as follows:</p>

    <div class="informalfigure medium"><a class="thumb" title="The correct layout has formal charges of zero whereas the incorrect form has -1 charges on C and O, and the carbon bound to oxygen has a +1 charge." rel="internal" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/28150/3b3308a7505b51566481e83d7bb8beef.jpg?revision=1"><img alt="The correct layout has formal charges of zero whereas the incorrect form has -1 charges on C and O, and the carbon bound to oxygen has a +1 charge." style="width: 241px; height: 121px;" width="241px" height="121px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/28150/3b3308a7505b51566481e83d7bb8beef.jpg?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=241&amp;height=121" /></a></div>
    </div>
    </li>
    <li class="qandaentry lt-chem-349446" id="av_1.0-ch04_s05_s05_s02_qs02_qd01_qa18_ans"> </li>
    <li class="qandaentry lt-chem-349446" id="av_1.0-ch04_s05_s05_s02_qs02_qd01_qa19_ans">
    <div class="answer">
    <p class="para lt-chem-349446">The most plausible Lewis structure for NO<sub>3</sub><sup>&minus;</sup> is:</p>

    <div class="informalfigure medium"><a class="thumb" title="Two oxygens with six valence electrons are bound to nitrogen through single bonds; another oxygen with four valence electrons bound to nitrogen through a double bond." rel="internal" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/28151/dd5d6be9d7c1d39d9fa5ca6c3a1588a7.jpg?revision=1"><img alt="Two oxygens with six valence electrons are bound to nitrogen through single bonds; another oxygen with four valence electrons bound to nitrogen through a double bond." style="width: 241px; height: 101px;" width="241px" height="101px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/28151/dd5d6be9d7c1d39d9fa5ca6c3a1588a7.jpg?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=241&amp;height=101" /></a></div>

    <p class="para lt-chem-349446">There are three equivalent resonance structures for nitrate (only one is shown), in which nitrogen is doubly bonded to one of the three oxygens. In each resonance structure, the formal charge of N is +1; for each singly bonded O, it is &minus;1; and for the doubly bonded oxygen, it is 0.</p>

    <p class="para lt-chem-349446">The following is an example of a Lewis structure that is <em>not</em> plausible:</p>

    <div class="informalfigure medium"><a class="thumb" title="Three oxygens with four valence electrons are bound to nitrogen through double bonds." rel="internal" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/28152/09255b6340088575909714e0d86417fb.jpg?revision=1"><img alt="Three oxygens with four valence electrons are bound to nitrogen through double bonds." style="width: 240px; height: 98px;" width="240px" height="98px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/28152/09255b6340088575909714e0d86417fb.jpg?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=240&amp;height=98" /></a></div>

    <p class="para lt-chem-349446">This structure nitrogen has six bonds (nitrogen can form only four bonds) and a formal charge of &ndash;1.</p>
    </div>
    </li>
    <li class="qandaentry lt-chem-349446" id="av_1.0-ch04_s05_s05_s02_qs02_qd01_qa20_ans"> </li>
    <li class="qandaentry lt-chem-349446" id="av_1.0-ch04_s05_s05_s02_qs02_qd01_qa21_ans">
    <div class="answer">
    <p class="para lt-chem-349446">With four S&ndash;O single bonds, each oxygen in SO<sub>4</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup> has a formal charge of &minus;1, and the central sulfur has a formal charge of +2. With two S=O double bonds, only two oxygens have a formal charge of &ndash;1, and sulfur has a formal charge of zero. Lewis structures that minimize formal charges tend to be lowest in energy, making the Lewis structure with two S=O double bonds the most probable.</p>
    </div>
    </li>
    <li class="qandaentry lt-chem-349446" id="av_1.0-ch04_s05_s05_s02_qs02_qd01_qa22_ans"> </li>
    <li class="qandaentry lt-chem-349446" id="av_1.0-ch04_s05_s05_s02_qs02_qd01_qa23_ans">
    <div class="answer">
    <p class="para lt-chem-349446">Yes. This is a reasonable Lewis structure, because the formal charge on all atoms is zero, and each atom (except H) has an octet of electrons.</p>

